---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const dividerCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'r1', label: 'R1', min: 100, max: 10000, step: 100, value: 1000, unit: 'Ω' },
    { name: 'r2', label: 'R2', min: 100, max: 10000, step: 100, value: 2000, unit: 'Ω' },
    { name: 'vin', label: 'Vin', min: 1, max: 24, step: 0.5, value: 9, unit: 'V' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'Vout = Vin·R2/(R1+R2)', expr: 'vin*r2/(r1+r2)', unit: 'V' },
    { label: 'current', expr: 'vin/(r1+r2)', unit: 'A' },
  ],
};

const lawsCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Ohm\'s law', note: 'V = IR. Voltage is the push, current the flow, resistance the friction. Three symbols carry an entire discipline.' },
    { math: '2 · Kirchhoff\'s current law', note: 'Charge does not accumulate at a node: current in = current out. That is conservation of charge, written for a junction.' },
    { math: '3 · Kirchhoff\'s voltage law', note: 'Around any closed loop the voltage rises and drops sum to zero — conservation of energy for a circuit walk.' },
    { math: '4 · Series and parallel simplify', note: 'Series resistances add; parallel conductances add. Most intimidating schematics collapse into these two moves.' },
    { math: '5 · Equivalent sources finish it', note: 'Any linear network seen from two terminals is just one source and one resistor — Thevenin and Norton\'s great compression.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/electrical-engineering/foundational/dc-circuit-analysis">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    A circuit is one of the friendliest systems in all of engineering: a closed world where
    exactly two quantities matter and exactly two conservation laws govern them. Voltage is
    electrical "height" — energy per unit charge, measured in joules per coulomb — and current
    is the flow of charge itself, coulombs per second. Resistance is the friction between them:
    how much push is needed to sustain a given flow. The water analogy is genuinely useful
    here: voltage is the pressure difference across a pipe run, current is the litres per
    second moving through it, and resistance is how narrow the pipe is. Pressurise a loop of
    pipework and the flow distributes itself automatically; the same happens, instantaneously
    and invisibly, in every wire you have ever touched.
  </p>
  <p>
    What makes circuit analysis remarkable is its completeness. Three laws — Ohm's law plus
    Kirchhoff's two conservation statements — are sufficient to solve <em>any</em> network of
    batteries and resistors, no matter how tangled, to exact algebraic precision. There is no
    statistical approximation, no empirical fudge: the schematic on a first-year problem set
    and the power-distribution network of a jetliner yield to the same three sentences. The
    skills this chapter builds — read the circuit, name the nodes, trust conservation — are
    the same ones a chip designer uses with a thousand nodes, except that the thousand-node
    version is done by software that automates precisely the recipe taught here.
  </p>
  <p>
    Direct current (DC) — steady flows, steady voltages — is where the reflexes are built.
    Alternating current adds capacitors, inductors and calculus on top; DC first ensures that
    when that complexity arrives, the conservation backbone underneath it is already
    automatic.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Ohm, Kirchhoff, and the network collapses" widget="equationStep" config={lawsCfg} />

  <h2>Ohm's law and Kirchhoff's two laws</h2>
  <p>
    Ohm's law, V = IR, states that the current through a resistor is proportional to the
    voltage across it, with the resistance as the constant of proportionality. Double the push,
    double the flow. It is a material property, not a deep law of nature — a diode or a
    lightbulb filament does not obey it — but metal wires at constant temperature follow it
    so faithfully that entire industries are built on it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Kirchhoff's current law (KCL) says the current flowing into any junction equals the current
    flowing out. This is conservation of charge: charge has nowhere to be created or stored at
    a connection point, so whatever arrives must leave. Kirchhoff's voltage law (KVL) says the
    sum of voltage rises and drops around any closed loop is zero. This is conservation of
    energy: walk a full circuit of the loop and you must return to your starting potential,
    just as a hiker returning to base camp has zero net elevation change. Battery rises pay
    for resistor drops, exactly, every time.
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked example shows the three laws cooperating. Take a 12 V battery feeding a
    100 Ω resistor in series with two 200 Ω resistors that are in parallel with each other.
    The parallel pair has resistance 200 ÷ 2 = 100 Ω, so the total is 200 Ω and Ohm's law
    gives a battery current of 12 ÷ 200 = 60 mA. KVL then splits the voltage: the series
    resistor drops 0.06 × 100 = 6 V, leaving 6 V across the pair. KCL finishes the job: 6 V
    across each 200 Ω branch drives 30 mA per branch, and indeed 30 + 30 = 60 mA — the books
    balance at every node. Note the division of labour: Ohm converts local voltage to local
    current, KVL constrains the voltages, KCL constrains the currents.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> current gets "used up" as it goes around a circuit. It does
    not — current is a flow rate of charge, and charge is conserved, so the same current that
    leaves the battery returns to it. What is consumed is <em>energy</em>: each coulomb
    delivers its cargo of joules to the resistors on the way around, emerging at lower
    potential. The river's flow rate is unchanged along its length even as its height drops
    over every waterfall.
  </p>

  <h2>The voltage divider: the circuit everyone owns</h2>
  <p>
    Two resistors in series split a source voltage in proportion to their resistances:
    Vout = Vin × R2 ÷ (R1 + R2). The derivation is two lines — one current through both
    resistors, KVL for the split — and the result is arguably the most-used circuit in
    existence. Sensors use it to turn resistance changes into voltage changes (a thermistor
    in one half makes a thermometer); microcontrollers use it to scale a battery's 4.2 V down
    to the 3.3 V an analogue input can read; volume controls in the analogue era were
    literally one adjustable divider.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="a two-resistor divider under your fingers" widget="sliderLab" config={dividerCfg} />

  <p>
    With the widget's default values — 9 V in, R1 = 1 kΩ, R2 = 2 kΩ — the output is
    9 × 2 ÷ 3 = 6 V and the current is a modest 3 mA. One warning deserves its own sentence:
    the divider formula is only exact when <em>nothing draws current from the tap</em>. The
    moment a load connects, part of R2's current is diverted, and the output sags — a preview
    of Thevenin's insight below.
  </p>

  <h2>Power: where the energy actually goes</h2>
  <p>
    Voltage and current together fix the rate of energy delivery: power equals voltage times
    current, P = VI. Substitute Ohm's law and two more forms appear — P = I²R and P = V²/R —
    which answer the two questions engineers actually ask: how hot will this resistor get, and
    how much current does this device really draw? Run the widget's default divider through
    the arithmetic: 9 V across 3 kΩ drives 3 mA, so the battery delivers 27 mW. The 1 kΩ
    resistor, dropping 3 V, dissipates 9 mW; the 2 kΩ, dropping 6 V, takes the other 18 mW.
    The books balance — 27 in, 27 out — because KVL is conservation of energy wearing a
    circuit's clothes.
  </p>
  <p>
    Those milliwatts explain a component you have seen and never questioned: the power rating
    printed on every resistor. A standard part survives a quarter watt; push 0.5 W through it
    and it cooks, darkens and drifts out of specification. The same arithmetic scaled up
    governs real infrastructure — a transmission line carrying megawatts loses I²R power as
    heat, which is precisely why grids transmit at enormous voltages: raising voltage cuts
    current in proportion, and the losses fall with the <em>square</em> of current. One line
    of algebra, P = I²R, is the reason the pylons outside your window carry 400,000 volts.
  </p>

  <h2>Nodal and mesh analysis: the general-purpose machines</h2>
  <p>
    Series-parallel simplification eventually fails — in a "ladder" network or a bridge,
    no two resistors are simply in series or parallel. The systematic fallback is
    <em>nodal analysis</em>: choose one node as ground (zero volts), write KCL at every other
    node in terms of the unknown node voltages, and solve the simultaneous equations. With n
    nodes there are n − 1 unknowns — modest algebra for a computer. <em>Mesh analysis</em> is
    its mirror image: assign a circulating current to each independent loop, write KVL around
    each loop, and solve for the loop currents. The two methods are equivalent in power;
    nodal analysis wins when the circuit has few nodes and many loops (which is most
    electronics), and it is the method modern simulators mechanise.
  </p>
  <p>
    Consider what nodal analysis does to a bridge circuit — four resistors forming a diamond,
    used in strain gauges and precision thermometers. No simplification applies, yet naming
    the two middle nodes and writing two KCL equations solves it completely. The same recipe
    extends without modification to networks with controlled sources, and SPICE — the
    simulation engine behind most chip design since the 1970s — is little more than nodal
    analysis performed at industrial scale, stamping each component into a giant matrix and
    solving it millions of times per simulation.
  </p>

  <h2>Thevenin and Norton: every network is one source and one resistor</h2>
  <p>
    The deepest compression in circuit theory is this: any network of sources and resistors,
    viewed from two terminals, behaves <em>exactly</em> like a single voltage source in
    series with a single resistance (Thevenin's equivalent), or equivalently a single current
    source in parallel with one resistance (Norton's). Not approximately — exactly, for any
    linear network. The Thevenin voltage is what you measure at the terminals with nothing
    connected; the Thevenin resistance is what you "see" looking back in, with all independent
    sources switched off.
  </p>
  <p>
    Work the widget's divider through it. Open-circuit output: 6 V. Looking back from the
    tap with the 9 V source replaced by a wire, R1 and R2 sit in parallel:
    (1000 × 2000) ÷ 3000 ≈ 667 Ω. Now attach a 1 kΩ load. The loaded output is
    6 × 1000 ÷ (1667) ≈ 3.6 V — a 40 percent sag that the naive divider formula never
    predicted. Thevenin's theorem explained the sag in one step, and it prescribes the cure:
    make the divider's resistances small compared with the load, shrinking the Thevenin
    resistance, so the tap holds its voltage. Every real source — battery, lab supply,
    sensor, audio amplifier — is honestly specified this way, by its open-circuit voltage and
    internal resistance, and every "loading effect" puzzle in measurement dissolves into two
    resistors in series.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> the Thevenin resistance is a physical resistor hidden
    somewhere in the circuit. It is not — it is a bookkeeping artefact, the lumped behaviour
    of the entire network as seen from outside. Open the box and you will find the original
    components untouched; close it and the two terminals genuinely cannot tell the difference
    between the full network and one 667 Ω resistor behind a 6 V source.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Two more principles round out the linear toolkit. <em>Superposition</em> says that in any
    linear circuit with several sources, each source's contribution can be computed alone —
    other sources switched off — and the answers added; it is why "noise pickup" and "signal"
    can be analysed separately and then combined. The <em>maximum power transfer theorem</em>
    says a load extracts the most power from a Thevenin source when the load resistance equals
    the Thevenin resistance, delivering 50 percent efficiency — fine for a radio antenna,
    deliberately avoided in a power grid, where matching the generator would waste half the
    plant. Note the subtlety the theorem hides: maximum <em>power</em> and maximum
    <em>voltage transfer</em> are different goals, and which one you design for depends on
    whether you are delivering energy or information.
  </p>
  <p>
    Beyond DC, the same skeleton carries everything. Replace resistance with impedance —
    resistance generalised to capacitors and inductors, whose opposition depends on frequency
    — and Ohm's and Kirchhoff's laws survive intact with complex numbers, which is what phasor
    AC analysis is. Nonlinearity is the honest boundary: diodes and transistors break Ohm's
    law, and the working method there is to linearise around an operating point — Thevenin
    equivalents again, computed at the bias point. Where the research edge lives: networks too
    large for exact solution (power grids under stochastic renewables), circuits whose
    <em>topology</em> switches millions of times per second in power converters, and
    thermal–electrical coupling, where resistance itself drifts with temperature until a
    "linear" circuit quietly stops being one.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NCEES FE Electrical and Computer exam specifications; Agarwal &amp; Lang, <em>Foundations of Analog and Digital Electronic Circuits</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
